ymbols (i.e., the symbols are still present in some node that is retained). So we need to simulate removal and check if required symbols are still present.\n- Also probably we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (since that would leave orphan children). \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" So we should only remove leaf nodes? Actually if we remove a node, its children would become children of its parent? In Perses, removal means the node is replaced by its children (flattening) or just deleted? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" typically removes a node and replaces it with its children (or deletes if leaf). But the condition \"node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a node that has any children that will be retained (because they'd become orphans). So we need to process in bottom-up order (deeper first), and remove nodes only if all their children have already been removed (or are being removed)? Actually if we process in descending depth, we will consider leaves first. When we remove a leaf, that's fine. For internal node, its children are deeper, so they have been processed already (some may have been removed, some retained). \"retained children\" means children that we have decided to keep. So we can only remove a node if it has no retained children (i.e., all its children have been removed already). That matches bottom-up: we remove leaves first, then internal nodes become leaves if all children removed.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So root is not removable anyway. Required symbol: if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it. Wait, \"required symbol\" may refer to the node that provides that symbol in the tree. For example, required_symbols list: [\"root\",\"s1\"] means we need at least one node with symbol \"root\" (that's root) and at least one node with sym