a node with required symbol that is duplicated and it's removable. In these fixtures, n4 is removable but symbol s1 required, so we don't remove it. But is that the intention? The phrase \"Never remove a required symbol\" is explicit. So yes.\n\nBut is there any case where we could remove a required symbol node if that symbol is still represented by another node? The instruction \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" suggests that we could remove it if the symbol is still represented elsewhere. However, the earlier hard rule \"Never remove a required symbol\" seems to prohibit it outright. I'm leaning towards the hard rule because it's stated as \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That list seems to be absolute prohibitions. The separate condition is an additional check that also must hold. So I'll implement: if node.symbol in required_symbols, then it cannot be removed under any circumstance, even if duplicates exist. This is simpler and matches the literal text.\n\nNow, what about nodes with retained children? We need to check each node's children that are still in retained set at the time of processing. Since we process bottom-up, we can maintain a set of removed IDs. When considering a node, its children are either already removed (in set) or still retained. If any child is not in removed set, then that child is retained, so we cannot remove the node.\n\nWe also need to ensure we don't attempt to remove the root (but root is not removable anyway). But also if root had children that are being removed, that's fine.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case: expect case to have \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings). Possibly also _case_nonce but ignore.\n2. Validate nodes: each must have id, parent (string or None), symbol, depth (int), removable (bool), utility (float) – but utility not used. Ensure root has parent null.\n3. Build dictionaries: id_to_node, children_by_parent.\n4. Determine initial retained set: all node IDs (since we start with all nodes). But we will remove nodes as we go; the set of currently retained nodes changes.\n5. Sort node ids by depth descending, then id ascending (string compare). Since depth is int, descending means larger depth first. For nodes at same depth, sort by id string.\n6. removed_nodes = [] (ordered list)\n7. For each node id in sorted list:\n   - node = id_to_node[id]\n   - if not node[\"removable\"]: continue\n   - if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols: continue (never remove required symbol)\n   - Check children: get children list from children_by_parent; for each child, if child id not in removed set (i.e., still retained), then cannot remove. If any child not removed, continue.\n   - Now check if all required symbols remain present after hypothetical removal. We can compute: after removal, the set of retained symbols is {id_to_node[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in retained_set if rid != node_id}. But we haven't removed yet. We need to see if removing this node would cause any required symbol to be missing. So for each sym in required_symbols, check if there exists any retained node (excluding this node) with that symbol. If for any required sym, no node other than this one has that symbol, then cannot remove.\n   - If all conditions satisfied, then remove: add node_id to removed set, append node_id to removed_nodes list. Also, since we remove node, we need to update retained set implicitly (we track removed set; retained_set = initial_all_ids - removed_set).\n8. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted set of all node ids that are not removed.\n9. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_nodes, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nBut careful: the check for retained children should consider children that are not in removed set at the time of processing. Since we process bottom-up, all children have been processed already (deeper depth). So if any child is still not removed, it means we chose to keep it (since it hasn't been removed). So condition holds.\n\nNow about the check for required symbols: we need to ensure that after removal, each required symbol appears in at least one remaining node. For nodes that are themselves a required symbol, we already skip them because we never remove required symbols. So this check may only be relevant for nodes that are not required symbol but might be the only node providing a required symbol? Actually no, because required symbols are attached to nodes that have that symbol. If a node is not a required symbol, it cannot be the only source of a required symbol. But consider: a node might have a child that, if removed, causes a required symbol to disappear? But we are only checking removal of the node itself, not its children. Its children are either already removed or will remain. If a child is a required symbol and we have removed it earlier? But we never remove required symbols. So the check might be unnecessary given the other rules. However, to be safe, we still perform the check.\n\nNow, what about the requirement \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" We'll check that case has nodes and required_symbols, that nodes list is non-empty, that root exists (parent null), that each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable. If any invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso note: the payload may have additional fields like \"_case_nonce\". We ignore.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) as per interface.\n\nWe'll write Python code that uses only standard library.\n\nPotential edge: ordering by depth descending, then id. IDs are strings; sorted order may not be numeric for \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n10\"? But we just do string sort. In examples, it's fine.\n\nNow, output format: Must return JSON-serializable dict. Use json module? Not needed, just return dict.\n\nNow, we need to produce the submission envelope: one JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is a string containing the Python source code.\n\nWe must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string.\n\nLet's craft the code.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate case\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    if \"nodes\" not in case or \"required_symbols\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"case missing required fields\")\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not nodes:\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes must be a non-empty list\")\n    # Build maps\n    id_to_node = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node.get(\"id\")\n        if nid is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"node missing id\")\n        id_to_node[nid] = node\n        parent = node.get(\"parent\")\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # Check root\n    root_nodes = [n for n in nodes if n.get(\"parent\") is None]\n    if len(root_nodes) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = root_nodes[0][\"id\"]\n    # Validate required fields per node\n    required_fields = [\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"]\n    for node in nodes:\n        for f in required_fields:\n            if f not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"node missing field {f}\")\n        if not isinstance(node[\"removable\"], bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"removable must be boolean\")\n        if not isinstance(node[\"depth\"], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"depth must be int\")\n    # Build depth map for sorting\n    depth_of = {nid: node[\"depth\"] for nid, node in id_to_node.items()}\n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, tie by id\n    all_ids = list(id_to_node.keys())\n    all_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-depth_of[nid], nid))\n    # Set of removed IDs\n    removed = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    # Helper to check if all required symbols are present\n    def required_symbols_present(exclude_id):\n        required = set(required_symbols)\n        for nid, node in id_to_node.items():\n            if nid in removed or nid == exclude_id:\n                continue\n            sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n            if sym in required:\n                required.discard(sym)\n                if not required:\n                    return True\n        return False\n\n    # Process nodes\n    for nid in all_ids:\n        node = id_to_node[nid]\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # Never remove required symbol\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Never remove root (should be non-removable anyway, but check)\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child in children.get(nid, []):\n            if child not in removed:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols remain\n        if not required_symbols_present(nid):\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        removed.add(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    # Retained set = all ids not removed\n    retained = sorted([nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained\n    }\n```\n\nBut wait: required_symbols_present function checks all required symbols are present in retained nodes excluding the candidate. That's correct.\n\nBut note: we used `removed` set to track removed nodes. Initially removed is empty. As we process, we add to removed. When checking required symbols present, we also exclude the candidate (exclude_id). But we also need to consider that some nodes may have been removed already in earlier steps, which are in removed set. That's